#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d53966-105e-5278-a8b5-9492f93a8bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389512} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e92543-ae84-5f62-8abd-4ba9d2be6651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389513} {:answer/id #uuid "0efb98d0-1949-559a-b001-eff539c4e7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389514} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccbc778-fe57-5c5c-9020-41b7af3786c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389515} {:answer/id #uuid "80dab713-4400-5029-a09b-df63f7d736b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0efb98d0-1949-559a-b001-eff539c4e7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389514} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f30222c-68be-5948-ada7-44c499e54de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3e82248-046d-51ec-a301-4e2c4f12e087" :question/math-academy-id "q-164409" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of an annulus $D$ centered at $O$ with inner radius $1\\,\\text{m},$ outer radius $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m},$ and mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{|xy|}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the ring passing through a point $3\\,\\text{m}$ from its center." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ the center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{|r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |}{{r}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )|.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )|\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-1)\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )|\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-1)\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $3\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+{3}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+9 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b007fc6-a9cf-594b-9130-ef77ac13f04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389519} {:answer/id #uuid "da47b9c2-8db5-5641-8844-3d6f057e449c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389520} {:answer/id #uuid "32a02b40-341b-55c1-bb30-36fc4d4ff206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389521} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89ca9e-8cf1-5b9c-a38d-3f29dfc620a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389522} {:answer/id #uuid "7881fdc3-f1e6-5655-83ea-d156cd6e11c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d89ca9e-8cf1-5b9c-a38d-3f29dfc620a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389522} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92fac85-7a9a-5915-90d4-a283e5eef049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38414465-84de-53ed-9f2d-540277ea9f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-164439" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18\\,⇒\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{sec}^{2}⁡t}{9}-\\frac{2{tan}^{2}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87b371d9-6172-5ac0-a105-c9887542f0a7" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043304f9-3a42-53ad-8538-3cea955fe040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389526} {:answer/id #uuid "8137e0c5-1440-5348-a3f6-f96860233599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389527} {:answer/id #uuid "6eea4516-42fb-5185-853e-429392d0e13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389528} {:answer/id #uuid "1720add8-c002-5c85-b431-35fa9200f452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389529} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4361d-c58f-5b04-80ef-1a4a55f43f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4361d-c58f-5b04-80ef-1a4a55f43f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389530} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebc0e1f-5e97-5290-8156-5abbc7c3ef25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9e45fcb-4a47-5dc3-ae3f-636d48328fa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164441" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{___},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=12{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,12{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $12{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87b371d9-6172-5ac0-a105-c9887542f0a7" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c57d5c-a186-5182-951a-b3f85487caeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389533} {:answer/id #uuid "1f864458-d395-5710-9718-008110d8e93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389534} {:answer/id #uuid "0021c5a0-2342-5a2a-adac-8476ca592221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389535} {:answer/id #uuid "1602f014-a557-5551-9efb-7af44884ed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389536} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cdf78f-30ac-56bf-91f8-1d994c3a2eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f864458-d395-5710-9718-008110d8e93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389534} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb314a1-d9e4-51ef-9694-2ebcbc7cbfc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca6b56-70a9-5f2f-9046-3f8fb6cda9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164450" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{___},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $8{z}^{2}=x.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $8{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89fbf42b-d171-5772-87ce-cf622b720963" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders With Alternative Orientations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd6eaa8-586c-5103-b0cc-92a8aaaf1a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281813} {:answer/id #uuid "2556d713-7643-5e10-b347-3ff594c56f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282020} {:answer/id #uuid "2b318cd8-3045-5788-9bb8-ab1134992947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282075} {:answer/id #uuid "56d2cf8c-c2ea-5087-9d96-d9a6dcd9d5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282432} {:answer/id #uuid "eafb0571-17a8-5784-a800-513c70030f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2556d713-7643-5e10-b347-3ff594c56f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282020} :answer-field/id #uuid "09284adb-1272-5cf4-81c3-aa20d058b0a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e28adc4e-c42a-55a7-ab38-db39626f07cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-164510" :question/problem "Identify the surface $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=\\sqrt{2},\\,c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "980f0e6e-b727-5884-9358-8f4cc871929f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389540} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd57ebb-6b0c-59bf-a69b-ea61588fa5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389541} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a90d4-f70c-5dbb-86f2-5da3595c4717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389542} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4ce062-cc41-523a-b403-73151b819608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389543} {:answer/id #uuid "3fca4567-695f-5afb-931c-d416f8324d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a90d4-f70c-5dbb-86f2-5da3595c4717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389542} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb03e48f-e4f7-56b0-b0ed-a15eec593f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a480e6-23f1-5a37-b498-2c012faa9753" :question/math-academy-id "q-164521" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡t-4{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d646eda-468b-5ae5-b7b0-0d53a948a808" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707fbf1b-bf7d-5995-b7cc-be64436a3be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389547} {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389548} {:answer/id #uuid "1adc692e-fe37-5019-893d-bd451359ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389549} {:answer/id #uuid "c552ac7b-ee72-589e-9065-cf0a29c3f734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389550} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ee24c-0ae3-5eec-973f-6e3492e4e1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389548} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea9a704-6834-5c1c-808b-11ae8ac08e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aefa1f59-5dce-5a67-8312-bce584cb45c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164522" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178ec7c6-6577-5d03-8700-c4e9795ab5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389554} {:answer/id #uuid "ddee70ca-4dee-5344-98b4-a5c1b8ec8eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389555} {:answer/id #uuid "11471da3-50f9-57e0-a336-84dfde593bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389556} {:answer/id #uuid "fb606d04-e907-587a-a715-2a4aa9dadf42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389557} {:answer/id #uuid "a44a32b3-5abd-5f8d-b7ab-3069ccce2ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44a32b3-5abd-5f8d-b7ab-3069ccce2ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389558} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e2f681-23d9-54e2-bb54-2bae73819f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c55ee03-8045-5fcf-97d2-93268d3a9359" :question/math-academy-id "q-164523" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d646eda-468b-5ae5-b7b0-0d53a948a808" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed22918-85fb-5c79-9620-31cecfbbc27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389561} {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389562} {:answer/id #uuid "974a57ae-291f-5842-95b3-64380db1d779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389563} {:answer/id #uuid "814a367c-72f0-5937-973d-1f16f3d1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389564} {:answer/id #uuid "487b991c-c37d-5069-8337-bf2040028617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389562} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d86410-beeb-5cce-9a2f-324bd43cac89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353926d7-638c-5e50-a022-4791f5ce8a3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164524" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{cos}^{2}⁡t}{25}+\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡t}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ac303b1-85ae-580e-869f-ca2e790928aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389568} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae8ab80-7b83-56e1-bd18-b63755b34c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389569} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a244e9-d9a2-5e95-ad44-bdc129bb6171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389570} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a3b3b-b8d5-52b2-babf-d29b970c7c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389571} {:answer/id #uuid "efe0cfb9-b029-526f-ae37-2cc3b0d3fee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a244e9-d9a2-5e95-ad44-bdc129bb6171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389570} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1ee068-9377-5972-afe2-7b833474280c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6585630a-9b94-5ba5-b2b2-756dcac0fca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-164583" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=2Z,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=2Z,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2Z,3Y,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a71980a-a780-533c-8292-ac7c714bcb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/364633d020e330780fab5ea588cca231ecd0e3d3c8e3e5516e4d6d0d990299c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389575} {:answer/id #uuid "30676351-f4d0-5879-a162-25816a196693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dae63d7007d115438139be51862d878deadd1ed300b3b870935c2cbe44edaef3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389576} {:answer/id #uuid "bf91d35b-3b4c-5341-8e3c-574c51f569f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46e48817af724645b2dde55f6aa55a48c44b5160de6aebec0b1da5cc7ef1e79c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389577} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0ada63-f751-502d-996f-08e36d9763eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389578} {:answer/id #uuid "bb18aec9-e801-5b41-b04e-dfaa22bace09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/adaef18865941a58a89a221c40f8e44aa6b95def80d12a08897c7be34ae7a9aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c0ada63-f751-502d-996f-08e36d9763eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389578} :answer-field/id #uuid "019c3753-a69c-52d8-a6b6-c6496d15982e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80a9d60b-2d00-5629-b0f1-438f9ef2a843" :question/math-academy-id "q-165980" :question/problem "![](images/61/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n  \n\n![](images/cf/cfe398e1ab7e5b4b487671772bf4b6127e8e141b25491f96c5cc32e77869dec0.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ad0535-275d-5569-9ac3-9c14fa99a5fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11ec7fbc5908465c69f2bea959f6164ab115b30f767260652658649bc7e9cf65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389582} {:answer/id #uuid "c97a6005-11ae-5bc5-9656-338b6e77d9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83ee71cbcf2456ce581e91624155d9528903c56c491debdbc3de392e7799720a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389583} {:answer/id #uuid "5731da45-7ae4-5755-be3e-c71696c160cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/1065343505af421e2a1fc06c9733dfc3a2354842daec12ac4bcc2d7935c8b1ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389584} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0e59b-b1ef-56d1-b881-1e0d2f7f1ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2d437d87e38ef73849987c358bc00fd589535c5045b193cdfa54ecc9601f37e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} {:answer/id #uuid "18a565a2-df94-546c-9080-271b1c1bf113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8ccfcbd3042f4504a2df8f51c82df1238fdc6c479fa99f6b4691543f8685ffba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0e59b-b1ef-56d1-b881-1e0d2f7f1ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2d437d87e38ef73849987c358bc00fd589535c5045b193cdfa54ecc9601f37e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fbff92d-a67a-5b5a-a0bc-1673af73baa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e46ce850-a38a-5466-abbe-3315ac65e081" :question/math-academy-id "q-167626" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the curve $y={7}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b2274f6e19ea02b6ebee1d9ac590c8186722be946f1d390ae4d27b2898d883e.png)\n\n- To plot $y={7}^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/d9/d900437ffd3ba0da1c6c25cbcff892e76929abe839fc8a49abea555c8163714f.png)\n\n- To plot the graph $y={7}^{x+2}-3,$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}$ vertically by $3$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c8f16780a5d9e3b655b9c7706fc664e9ef944d728d070c9cca886ac05b34504.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91d68c4-e924-5c0b-8892-337b390a9278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cadd20667d93ddc9058703331d2442573b4febc0eb79b0c50749490a859d7006.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389589} {:answer/id #uuid "d252b9b0-032b-5d42-9ac1-9f37846b1397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4442c3b03668bf3d4b8090c56c4ad083e85aa4399a0326255fe2322f2dcc729b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389590} {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389591} {:answer/id #uuid "b9429aad-0aa1-50e2-95f4-b8ee60295084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b934a5fcadb46619e6c3631e6f9d78c8a0f72beeb023f9696c01422503188586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389592} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ba8eb-d544-5202-91c3-233917e73327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed323df68b293af1fa8ec575d4dca23f16fe01865b01c3fa9817eead7b66fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389591} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba892752-d68e-5265-b5cc-644accea6428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2385ee-c24f-5a4c-9712-f5717ae06cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167668" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e9/e992d80b1245e6fad1c1e2968c396c51ef863d56d36c184592f8025fc450fa99.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa0778c338e9b57aca82057edc1b984e051d05a3f5413c2b1c70c3874fcf063e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/25/25b4ef6f489f19d81b1d54a3240faf5e99f20a9463aa2ce9bd2e39796bf39cb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "998d5775-3897-58a7-be81-33df4635714b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389595} {:answer/id #uuid "17279587-9d61-57c4-8852-81887e7ed546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389596} {:answer/id #uuid "501a3e32-591b-5c01-87d8-50757934eb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389597} {:answer/id #uuid "36576214-4761-5d8f-82d6-057ab39a512e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389598} {:answer/id #uuid "3021ffd2-da96-524d-a723-0f4e25aada2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501a3e32-591b-5c01-87d8-50757934eb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389597} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccec1e8e-10f0-535e-b2c6-f8c6222c9dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ff5758f-22cd-54b1-923c-320e4412a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-168100" :question/problem "If\n$u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e353de59-45ed-5d3e-a3c7-c62ccb8bfee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389602} {:answer/id #uuid "189f5878-608c-523c-8af3-61b336e7fea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389603} {:answer/id #uuid "cae29720-9bdf-5675-b7dc-87fb52dc9fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389604} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e89d64-16cb-5f6e-924f-288db18c0bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/de/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389605} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e82089-b886-5a96-8c4e-a2c7d256121e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e89d64-16cb-5f6e-924f-288db18c0bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/de/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e761f31-f26b-54c2-90ef-d2fbb7506ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/1b/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4246cb3-6d9d-59d8-8e0b-61f2720827fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=c[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,c\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389609} {:answer/id #uuid "d6208dda-7e95-5383-969d-fe555641189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389610} {:answer/id #uuid "94dc3de2-a565-524c-89ba-e336441f2e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389611} {:answer/id #uuid "35921fae-079f-5be5-9367-307c773d93e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=c[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,c\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389612} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d9430d-a47f-5e0d-aede-4a2781504493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{1}t}+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94dc3de2-a565-524c-89ba-e336441f2e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389611} :answer-field/id #uuid "2caff293-2bb1-50a6-9bae-17c548a69250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7beb869-f92b-5876-8cc3-7148caaba07a" :question/math-academy-id "q-168498" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031fcf4c-44d6-5f96-acb1-353d67b1fad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389616} {:answer/id #uuid "8a74ffc8-d62f-510a-82cc-c909e16d7fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389617} {:answer/id #uuid "8513d342-7c71-558c-bb5b-2d0b8669c8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389618} {:answer/id #uuid "89d6b4d2-63a0-58db-b23d-fe914857704c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389619} {:answer/id #uuid "ede6c55e-98c0-5183-847e-807c623a05f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8513d342-7c71-558c-bb5b-2d0b8669c8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389618} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a4ed42-4ec3-5cae-83cc-1a4e796d7a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d561dbfb-7ee3-58dc-8154-27aa2bc8812a" :question/math-academy-id "q-168916" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n6{c}_{1}=12\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+2{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+1[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fde92cf-da54-5afe-9f1a-b3d21d6bf290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389623} {:answer/id #uuid "87090451-e0b4-56e6-a0c2-708d60d4f12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4}+3{e}^{6} \\\\ -{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389624} {:answer/id #uuid "306f0ec4-5a23-5893-a77a-0c4ccd9d70a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4} \\\\ 5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389625} {:answer/id #uuid "51a41736-479c-5cbe-a6a5-f920a289f460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ {e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389626} {:answer/id #uuid "8e43a50c-201c-59de-8f0a-2df24646008e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{6} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4}+{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fde92cf-da54-5afe-9f1a-b3d21d6bf290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389623} :answer-field/id #uuid "207eb684-605f-5bc6-8756-8f315ee86796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048c4d19-f776-56af-aac6-30cdbc559443" :question/math-academy-id "q-168917" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(2)}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532e6508-61ba-5eba-82b1-83db21c9fb33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389630} {:answer/id #uuid "ec421350-152f-515c-b132-c1fa1e0e5abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389631} {:answer/id #uuid "ca561a00-f3ed-5ae3-839e-5bc22f1eedf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389632} {:answer/id #uuid "ef26ee6a-fc1d-56a4-bcdc-c497d093b074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389633} {:answer/id #uuid "114db9ca-3afb-5efc-a291-5ffafc5a51cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114db9ca-3afb-5efc-a291-5ffafc5a51cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389634} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa134da7-c53e-5d34-95ff-f5e5662ff313" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "075fe6de-58ec-5896-b63e-8c0d09f0a158" :question/math-academy-id "q-170378" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fcc9d7-da26-566b-892f-8ca0dccbf100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389637} {:answer/id #uuid "a86affbe-00af-5daa-9d5d-d6b4c732cb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389638} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b263a-4cb7-517f-88b9-8ca97ec6707e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389639} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f8b750-04ea-55ad-b825-30a6ece0925e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389640} {:answer/id #uuid "0ceac102-859c-568b-8eb7-f0592356b505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33fcc9d7-da26-566b-892f-8ca0dccbf100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389637} :answer-field/id #uuid "205f15e5-0108-5b4a-888b-1b37b338482c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8daa1f82-58aa-5738-bbe7-9b509869abcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-170411" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a6205e-2924-5cbb-a37c-53602e79f359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389644} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0747c-a561-57f7-8630-821f8a80e665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389645} {:answer/id #uuid "b8eed71d-054b-5407-9d91-8e57f3cc52dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389646} {:answer/id #uuid "21a52435-a11d-5b4c-bfb8-496323584d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389647} {:answer/id #uuid "8a78e66d-9ed7-5211-b359-0bca69ed1942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0a6205e-2924-5cbb-a37c-53602e79f359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389644} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab1c9da-4432-585e-811e-f80370a2d619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f869ed0-df60-5562-b5c5-b1ddb2293f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-170504" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e97a677c-7cd3-5ab4-a853-3e58af95ef68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389651} {:answer/id #uuid "d626e8f2-6f92-5b4c-b77d-4433976b4f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389652} {:answer/id #uuid "388c0d7a-2358-5cd2-b7be-8784a5eb4f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389653} {:answer/id #uuid "5bee8a5d-5168-5c30-886c-aa5b72b8ba65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389654} {:answer/id #uuid "34eaf1be-fb4e-53d6-9abd-f05704e7c071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bee8a5d-5168-5c30-886c-aa5b72b8ba65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389654} :answer-field/id #uuid "807b9eea-f22c-56a9-981a-517ef2430f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "546ae5ae-d06c-5386-b960-6a8c9d016f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-170514" :question/problem "The minimum value of $Q(x)=8{x}_{1}^{2}-30{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-50{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $4{x}_{1}^{2}+25{x}_{2}^{2}=100,$ is $-250.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=5$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{5}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -75 \\\\ -30 & -100\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-250,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{min}=-250.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-250:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-250I) & =[\\begin{aligned}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{aligned}]+250[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}450 & -150 \\\\ -150 & 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-250I))y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814686d0-8b86-5eea-bdb8-93024ca0a0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧(X∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389657} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c2d545-f036-5411-b332-04fc26845144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389658} {:answer/id #uuid "0f404bae-6c14-5ef1-b00e-2af2baa4d9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389659} {:answer/id #uuid "fa380091-ad0a-557d-b58a-640106654e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389660} {:answer/id #uuid "c60192dd-7fa2-59da-ac7f-90d620615f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨(X∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa380091-ad0a-557d-b58a-640106654e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389660} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc2f853-19fc-5b37-9ee8-34daca264444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8b4f09f-c207-5e3e-880a-0207f5440924" :question/math-academy-id "q-170727" :question/problem "$x$ is positive **and** prime.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $X(x):$ $x$ is positive\n\n- $Y(x):$ $x$ is prime" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∧Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71006df3-094b-544a-8d16-aac2cd8f3223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∨G" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389664} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ad243f-0f35-5ec2-8ec0-8002c544b1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∧G" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389665} {:answer/id #uuid "8c521dcf-8c85-5590-bbe0-f4ea54692826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∧(G∧F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389666} {:answer/id #uuid "dc23a41f-2a8c-5280-8a84-ec9e53692ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(F∧G)∨(F∧G)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389667} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb348b5-55ca-5bc3-a13c-7902825b88c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∧(F∨G)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9ad243f-0f35-5ec2-8ec0-8002c544b1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∧G" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389665} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3a206-f750-5622-89b6-8d0f7fa111af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017fc845-8be5-5798-b79f-7d6c9b39bc09" :question/math-academy-id "q-170730" :question/problem "$x$ is divisible by $2$ **and** $3.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $F(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $G(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $3$" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is divisible by }2}_{⏟}}_{F}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is divisible by }3}_{⏟}}_{G},\n$$\n\nthat is, $F∧G.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93bc0caa-2352-5d02-9ccf-7267dff4be2c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Compound Statements in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f0507d-20d4-5775-b373-d900d8b96779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧(X∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389671} {:answer/id #uuid "74edb7a8-7861-5d22-ad52-5bf2049659b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389672} {:answer/id #uuid "79bde983-110b-51ba-9196-948da1351529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389673} {:answer/id #uuid "98ad621e-6a51-525b-87d3-68cb43e74fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389674} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7c8e26-e02a-5580-a252-6eeef8520438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨(X∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98ad621e-6a51-525b-87d3-68cb43e74fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389674} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a5c6cf-be89-504a-bea5-37363120d54a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c84caa5-0818-5b64-a106-3aa76c7b323f" :question/math-academy-id "q-170738" :question/problem "Express the statement \"$4$ is even and positive\" in symbolic form, where $X=$ \"$4$ is even\" and $Y=$ \"$4$ is positive.\"" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nNote that the given sentence is a shortened version of the following full sentence:\n\n$4$ is even and $4$ is positive.\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{4\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{4\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∧Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ecedf59-0fe7-58a6-aacc-d640ce086d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨G)∧O" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389678} {:answer/id #uuid "37095068-5e98-5d10-aee2-8a222d0c5f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨G)∨O" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389679} {:answer/id #uuid "5f65e393-af07-54d7-923a-4aa04bffbe52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∧G)∨O" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389680} {:answer/id #uuid "36d653b3-02a6-563f-b51f-cd0ec885e6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∨(G∧O)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389681} {:answer/id #uuid "32e34ad5-e525-5f03-a5ae-836fe1bc0fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∧(G∨O)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ecedf59-0fe7-58a6-aacc-d640ce086d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨G)∧O" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389678} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae36450-86c0-5a2d-ac26-cd9a41374e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3853e4f6-383a-57dd-8a21-07835f3f0f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-170755" :question/problem "$n$ is negative **or** greater than $10,$ **and** $n$ is odd.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $N(n):$ $n$ is negative\n\n- $G(n):$ $n$ is greater than $10,$\n\n- $O(n):$ $n$ is odd" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$  is negative or greater than $10.$  This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is negative }}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{n\\text{ is greater than }10}_{⏟}}_{G},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨G.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is odd. This corresponds to $O.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is negative }\\text{ or }\\text{ greater than }10}_{⏟}}_{N∨G}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{n\\text{ is odd }}_{⏟}}_{O},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(N∨G)∧O.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5bd766-d624-54e3-945e-7ae37b83886a" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0035e6e-f36a-5da1-a062-615e7f0ab3c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3 \\mid 18$ and $11$ is not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057001} {:answer/id #uuid "0b775ec8-6ca9-5db2-8064-a450b230418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3 \\mid 18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057002} {:answer/id #uuid "101774e8-84db-52ee-9a64-592220a96e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3⧸ \\mid 18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057003} {:answer/id #uuid "37228b0c-4acf-5470-b487-ce615401948e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3⧸ \\mid 18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057004} {:answer/id #uuid "698ca85f-1919-5143-8622-5c257aae85d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3 \\mid 18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057005}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c555a8be-5736-508c-a3a8-50d0c1ffb86f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057000}] :question/id #uuid "711d64e0-f4d6-5c58-a54c-d6327695bb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171016" :question/problem "$3 \\mid 18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de943075-23ed-550c-9348-3beb6a4a1c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389685} {:answer/id #uuid "8b02c54e-2d06-5289-a555-a7919b41a546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389686} {:answer/id #uuid "b98c7d3c-5c1d-57c7-bf18-b70064ffde0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389687} {:answer/id #uuid "5c092ed6-6792-5c60-919a-67c8b2cefe42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389688} {:answer/id #uuid "3560a2b5-61fc-59ef-b588-2de0e12fe534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b02c54e-2d06-5289-a555-a7919b41a546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389686} :answer-field/id #uuid "566667e2-62c5-546d-bc15-bcb677d80fec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab5fd68-e114-5907-8def-2141c3cce14d" :question/math-academy-id "q-171257" :question/problem "$X⇒Y\\,≡$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nX⇒Y\\,≡\\,¬X∨Y\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4a372ba-1e69-54c2-a8f1-50011e4561ea" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Plain English Sentence With Two Quantifiers as a Formal Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb6dcc3c-4406-5ab6-b586-75658d4ccfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,a,b\\in Z,\\,(ab∣a)∧\\,(ab∣b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389691} {:answer/id #uuid "741bced2-4905-5c77-b304-073b236c297e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,a,b\\in Z,\\,(a∣ab)∧\\,(b∣ab)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389692} {:answer/id #uuid "39ffe32a-20e3-5b48-a821-ea322fdecf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,a,b\\in Z,\\,(ab∣a)∧\\,(ab∣b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389693} {:answer/id #uuid "4682878d-6d48-5151-82ce-ee1d5ef0e1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,a,b\\in Z,\\,(a∣ab)∧\\,(b∣ab)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389694} {:answer/id #uuid "b055a666-48aa-561e-a3b9-127c91b92dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,Z,\\,(a∣ab)∧\\,(b∣ab)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "741bced2-4905-5c77-b304-073b236c297e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,a,b\\in Z,\\,(a∣ab)∧\\,(b∣ab)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389692} :answer-field/id #uuid "b938bd8a-674e-5560-baee-2b19ddb2229f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "331ac626-4716-5c06-ac45-245518864cb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-172009" :question/problem "The product of any two integers is divisible by those two integers.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to two integers. So, by denoting our numbers as $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n∀\\,a,b\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(a∣ab)\\,∧\\,(b∣ab)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∀a,b\\in Z,\\,(a∣ab)\\,∧\\,(b∣ab)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5ab572-f91e-503a-9ece-7b3c890d41c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Plain English Sentence as a Formal Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f17afc5-3f3e-502c-bdbd-d13962f439f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in R,∀\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389697} {:answer/id #uuid "31b706f5-a5cd-5d05-af5e-a5d0b6178228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in Z,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389698} {:answer/id #uuid "48c96aee-17d1-5cc1-a05c-36a74a7b15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389699} {:answer/id #uuid "e2655d09-24af-5208-98f9-157a4cc139ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389700} {:answer/id #uuid "96b593c9-e876-5c31-bc11-6d09d6cfc70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48c96aee-17d1-5cc1-a05c-36a74a7b15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389699} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6764cab-0539-5282-bd08-d80806e0c606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9df6de9-724a-51c9-8b20-e0553a9a581a" :question/math-academy-id "q-172013" :question/problem "The square of every real number is non-negative.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"every\" indicates we should use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to real numbers. So, by denoting a real number as $x,$ we have that \n\n$$\n∀\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows: \n\n$∀\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4a372ba-1e69-54c2-a8f1-50011e4561ea" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Plain English Sentence With Two Quantifiers as a Formal Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903f44db-d40e-5110-9760-881666935a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in R,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389704} {:answer/id #uuid "3aed95fc-cd21-532d-a660-26a49394d468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389705} {:answer/id #uuid "49e536b6-6d3b-5721-ba08-baff48bdd3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389706} {:answer/id #uuid "624aec29-7781-5648-b5a8-feaf11389d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389707} {:answer/id #uuid "3f403f9a-885f-5d1c-a218-88e879342684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,∀\\,x\\in R,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e536b6-6d3b-5721-ba08-baff48bdd3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,x<n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389706} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe2e530-0ab5-5ea1-8386-decf62114ee9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "325a8254-9661-5da1-94ce-81f6e382d9d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-172020" :question/problem "Given any real number, there exists an integer greater than it.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The words \"there exists\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $∃.$\n\n- The sentence refers to a real number and an integer. So, by denoting our numbers as $x$ and $n,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx<n\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,x<n$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc2780ae-9b3a-5b4e-8bb6-54182f2f861b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Implicit Quantification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318e6947-a97e-5c0c-98e5-62329ae4629e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,m\\in N,\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}=n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389711} {:answer/id #uuid "bbea2909-6211-532c-8ad9-d5109ee79161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃\\,m\\in N,\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}={n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389712} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf7c031-9068-5c4c-9e0d-78348f6962f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,100={n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389713} {:answer/id #uuid "0404c4c6-6289-5160-a30f-b7eae8a48f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}=n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389714} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2dcd54-0589-53d0-b8b2-ceeb42eee87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}={n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2dcd54-0589-53d0-b8b2-ceeb42eee87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}={n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c499b3d0-3890-5f95-bbdc-a6a512cd5b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1781644f-025a-57ae-b6b3-6c61df644c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-172268" :question/problem "Natural powers of $100$ are perfect squares.\n\nExpress the above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as \n\nFor any natural number $m,$ we have that ${100}^{m}$ is a perfect square.\n\nNow, \"${100}^{m}$ is a perfect square\" means that\n\nThere exists an integer number $n$ such that ${100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\nFor any natural number $m,$ there exists an integer $n$ such that ${100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7943a26b-d4fa-5ce1-bc47-5ea4873fc367" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Predicate Expressed Using Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca78fccf-8d97-515a-8b1d-984bd5dc39bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∉ ∅" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194965} {:answer/id #uuid "00ab8e7f-15da-51b8-b7b7-8d842f79bd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∉ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194966} {:answer/id #uuid "0b622f64-e0d2-5fe9-afdf-d5be845b52d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ C∖R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194967} {:answer/id #uuid "149e0b44-85cb-5ed3-94e8-067c8963a679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ ∅" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194968} {:answer/id #uuid "69f4251e-1d8d-5606-8e06-8c7e57489cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∉ Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194969}] :answer-field/id #uuid "26b017ab-dc35-51ae-b9f6-3bcd8c7839d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194964}] :question/id #uuid "3dc836e6-a152-59c0-a817-45e5b70cbfb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-175315" :question/problem "$x ∈ R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a4d09b-80d2-5f19-b45f-05317454b827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L∨(E∧P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389718} {:answer/id #uuid "b7971a96-a768-5469-8f3e-a3e783e9b4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(L∨E)∧P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389719} {:answer/id #uuid "36e425de-0e80-5a0b-86e9-e872104cb553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L∨E∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389720} {:answer/id #uuid "a6250575-900c-5544-b04c-18b583f55c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(L∧E)∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389721} {:answer/id #uuid "c70cd278-4590-58e6-9b87-682c16024743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L∧E∧P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e425de-0e80-5a0b-86e9-e872104cb553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L∨E∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389720} :answer-field/id #uuid "0814b16b-b84a-50f0-89ee-6bebde44015c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de46e6cd-5305-5c67-9e7b-98d46fc43965" :question/math-academy-id "q-175380" :question/problem "$x$ is less than $1,$ **or** $x$ is even **or**  prime.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $L(x):$ $x$ is less than $1$\n\n- $E(x):$ $x$ is even\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime" :question/worked-solution "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:**  $x$ is less than $1.$ This corresponds to $L.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is even or prime. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is even}}_{⏟}}_{E}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is prime}}_{⏟}}_{P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $E∨P.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is less than }1}_{⏟}}_{L}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is even or prime}}_{⏟}}_{E∨P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $L∨E∨P.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd3d59c-34e0-571f-95f2-3d05c4854017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x^{2}}{C - x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098820} {:answer/id #uuid "41bbf951-aa26-52c9-8709-c206091beb9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x}{C - 2x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098821} {:answer/id #uuid "4a875131-a5ef-555c-88fe-10c3cbba4761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x^{2}}{C - 3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098822} {:answer/id #uuid "69e6fa6a-384a-5822-b0a4-50fc85d898f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x}{C - x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098823} {:answer/id #uuid "75b3114b-d9d2-5df8-bc13-d0be71f33055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x}{C - 3x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098824}] :answer-field/id #uuid "80134138-d064-5cfb-9406-b08905da5331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098819}] :question/id #uuid "ffe3c5eb-c32b-557c-96a7-7ea03bab3fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17544" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0343692c-a358-56cc-ad31-3caa8d37ff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{5-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389725} {:answer/id #uuid "afa1e3c3-2e37-5ac7-8e1d-fc0face1926d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389726} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bf87b0-1d98-5426-8046-486143ddf661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389727} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc552c2-315e-5895-974e-defd34743230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{2-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389728} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd0c96-757f-554b-9e83-9a5b1a7e579e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{3-2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa1e3c3-2e37-5ac7-8e1d-fc0face1926d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389726} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b6edc8e-e15a-59f4-94d1-b264af8881fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5c02985-b98e-5a6d-903a-448a7295c82b" :question/math-academy-id "q-17556" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}+x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation*\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\n*using the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\\,dx & =-\\int {x}^{2}dx \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nNow, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition $y(1)=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{3}{C-1}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{5}{2}-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "910c3b29-8411-5178-aa08-da5973f17ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098850} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcb3f1d-f9fb-5dc7-b71d-39c214a17018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x + 4}{4x - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098851} {:answer/id #uuid "bc723036-5cf4-5a5a-9486-3aeacd5767d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x + 7}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098852} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fde202-15df-576e-82af-03b07d36e9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5x}{5x - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098853} {:answer/id #uuid "24f873ae-89d3-51b6-9a73-a9e200f4f395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x - 6}{x - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098854}] :answer-field/id #uuid "91d6510d-8ad6-560a-9d54-6c59a126adf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098849}] :question/id #uuid "1ccfb013-777e-5e8a-8a81-f7a1a7ca50df" :question/math-academy-id "q-17559" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29714588-6335-5a3d-9e8e-6b9fe2ff989d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389732} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fac748-5f68-5d84-84b4-0c2626ded647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6fac748-5f68-5d84-84b4-0c2626ded647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389733} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae63eeb-0ec3-5e40-b42d-72190c300318" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389731} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02c34d4-7b0c-5b65-9668-0c6a9af1fbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389735} {:answer/id #uuid "6adc1873-94c6-563f-9c49-fd5fb9a16778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adc1873-94c6-563f-9c49-fd5fb9a16778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389736} :answer-field/id #uuid "574a91d1-41fa-5456-a8c8-197945bd4223" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5633f3b-27c0-5c52-8d2f-ac92c09e1805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389738} {:answer/id #uuid "610c67e2-723f-5d90-b263-aa6df2457a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "610c67e2-723f-5d90-b263-aa6df2457a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389739} :answer-field/id #uuid "0617aa93-4a98-5969-9378-7be2f4f05f2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dac36ef-0828-520a-adc1-0e3c49c1f35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-175913" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\sqrt{5}\\in R}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{10\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∧B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is true. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\sqrt{5}\\in R}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{10\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233361df-2934-52b6-8409-87787fbc9ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389742} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfa55d8-486d-51be-a4f2-c7daf80570a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233361df-2934-52b6-8409-87787fbc9ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389742} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b17155-b098-59ce-8697-4b721be59f4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b46c0c9-5b08-5a0c-9c9e-0dbe02dfb5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389745} {:answer/id #uuid "401f28b4-450c-5fb5-92f6-b60aac6b8e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b46c0c9-5b08-5a0c-9c9e-0dbe02dfb5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0296d9ca-6449-57dc-9056-e88a340f66de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389744} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9109cf47-1309-5cc5-920f-0c4a9f90d70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389748} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8dd9c7-c93a-5e58-94d6-b052dd96294c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9109cf47-1309-5cc5-920f-0c4a9f90d70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389748} :answer-field/id #uuid "2765f9f5-199f-582b-9383-88bd1a2c2e34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9797c81c-4349-5761-91d7-aa11ddbab4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-175917" :question/problem "$$\n{{{1,2}\\cap {3}={1,2,3}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{9\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∧B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is false, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{1,2}\\cap {3}={1,2,3}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{9\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{false}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c15e521-f848-5d9c-ba06-00550277b8cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Complex Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb1dd0a-0ca1-5bdb-b32b-cb9c9615c408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389752} {:answer/id #uuid "6662b295-cb2f-5d75-aff5-9866f57c1820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb1dd0a-0ca1-5bdb-b32b-cb9c9615c408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389752} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1707f6-326f-5b61-a3f2-edca4616828d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248fd247-5725-5ce1-b32e-e7fbd5906215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389755} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9b3c79-68bf-5b28-8c82-a8e10fb1894a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "248fd247-5725-5ce1-b32e-e7fbd5906215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389755} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b83112-e0d0-50f7-a4bc-ee9374e99f50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df8f096-3a1f-5588-ab63-a01ec7657b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389758} {:answer/id #uuid "b7adf72b-4a8c-5254-9600-81eff3449b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7df8f096-3a1f-5588-ab63-a01ec7657b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389758} :answer-field/id #uuid "9969359a-3932-5955-a5d7-3796ec7d66c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001c067c-108b-5fca-a346-5a02614cd60f" :question/math-academy-id "q-175921" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\overset{P}{\\overset{⏞}{3-1=2}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{2\\text{ is negative}}}}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∧\\,Q}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\pi \\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∧Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∧Q)∨R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∧Q$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∨R & ≡\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "adbddf14-3aca-50dd-80f8-4146193c91ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of Predicate's Negation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84cfd2d-954e-54e6-ab18-5a1919d57564" :answer/typ