e "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390014} {:answer/id #uuid "5441d706-07b0-56cb-991c-117547385c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78b9394-e9f3-578e-8197-7a639b2790c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390014} :answer-field/id #uuid "e877c35a-91ca-54b6-838b-aabad485149e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e5f0d1-6ada-5ee8-a249-13c01b751042" :question/math-academy-id "q-182480" :question/problem "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,R\\,$ | $\\,¬P⇔R\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add a column for $¬P:$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,R\\,$ | $\\,¬P\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for $¬P⇔R.$ \n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,R\\,$ | $\\,¬P\\,$ | $\\,¬P⇔R\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e85e1e6-6a11-52e6-96d3-a768ced940d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Involving Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1242503-2cfd-5a6d-885d-d98e20fd2909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P⇒¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390018} {:answer/id #uuid "5d38e57e-0034-5dd9-a57b-7bd7718fc14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∧(P∧Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390019} {:answer/id #uuid "aff1ba4d-72c7-56c5-88ab-eb45126bed35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)∨(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390020} {:answer/id #uuid "3e745bf8-7edb-5ffa-b4cd-36f447cc7ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)∧Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390021} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ac1124-0415-519d-a404-12c337f5f986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9ac1124-0415-519d-a404-12c337f5f986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390022} :answer-field/id #uuid "dba9ffc3-357d-506c-9202-fec472b11180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "922eca05-7895-590f-aa05-4c1007a898ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-182520" :question/problem "$¬(¬P⇔Q)≡$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇔Q)≡¬[(¬P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒¬P)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒¬P)≡¬[(¬¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨¬P)]\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨¬P)]≡¬[(P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨¬P)]\n$$\n\nNow, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨¬P)] & ≡¬(P∨Q)∨¬(¬Q∨¬P) \\\\  & ≡¬(P∨Q)∨¬¬(Q∧P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we again simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(P∨Q)∨¬¬(Q∧P) & ≡¬(P∨Q)∨(Q∧P) \\\\  & ≡¬(P∨Q)∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe8eb0bd-6d37-5eb8-98fd-796b2af992f7" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equivalence Truth Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2db2d8-b4eb-55d8-a90c-7ac7c8cc32a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sometimes false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390025} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c7af80-2a03-5acb-8ed0-4bcb677f9c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sometimes true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390026} {:answer/id #uuid "30e90dd2-cd04-5639-85c0-102422a20adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390027} {:answer/id #uuid "683f0f0e-96a5-5002-81bd-7349a286246c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "683f0f0e-96a5-5002-81bd-7349a286246c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390028} :answer-field/id #uuid "7176b171-88bc-5eb0-8c23-0863a35fb450" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9975dc7b-5885-54a3-951c-a9d988a2e21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-182530" :question/problem "If $A$ is false and $B$ is false, then $A⇔B$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A=\\text{F}$ and $B=\\text{F},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{F}⇔\\text{F}) \\\\  & =\\text{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35e42262-e78c-5553-acfe-739684165622" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table for an Equivalence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fdc487-d040-5743-b3b8-c780f614d788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390031} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5533b6-e82b-5f29-9f92-b1cb1776d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5533b6-e82b-5f29-9f92-b1cb1776d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390032} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fade381-ee96-535c-8301-e5a205c13f27" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d64c980f-66f4-524b-9633-bf6f12bce849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390034} {:answer/id #uuid "30ee7c89-085f-5f3c-9d68-7d75690c6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30ee7c89-085f-5f3c-9d68-7d75690c6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id