7592186390072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c4383dc-6539-5c11-b62d-b47e616236a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(k-1)(k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390071} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8896cfb-de25-5559-9b2a-c5139b4ed458" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "296a5d38-c04a-5f80-a77b-434cec0ad980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390074} {:answer/id #uuid "231a862b-df1e-5522-94de-54fafc330fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390075} {:answer/id #uuid "93f5f515-bc83-5870-b5fd-642183a290c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "296a5d38-c04a-5f80-a77b-434cec0ad980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390074} :answer-field/id #uuid "65aa8026-2b95-57b5-b7ea-a0b5ff121778" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc226d8-01b0-5028-a262-eb884b9186e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-182624" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(2).$ To do this, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}(2)(2-1)(2+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}k(k-1)(k+1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=2,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1) & =\\frac{1}{3}(k+1)((k+1)-1)((k+1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}k(k+1)(k+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e1c01fd-dea0-5761-9499-d622a34fea3e" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table With Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e065c5c-f46f-5cad-b297-e98b69179978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390079} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec4fa4a-fe51-51f9-b673-29cf3048b64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec4fa4a-fe51-51f9-b673-29cf3048b64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390080} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de61de5-4361-56a9-9b1d-8a8c93c89b7f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0571136-b5e1-5829-9c13-2506b63f4083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390082} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bb62d5-ace8-5c89-9167-d944f1a2e008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bb62d5-ace8-5c89-9167-d944f1a2e008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390083} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac76c155-962d-5f15-bacb-e840964127cc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a660111-8853-589d-8123-eb5e5162ee