eld/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd17997-85e4-5a31-b517-0cda4e28bff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390116} {:answer/id #uuid "8de35645-2d60-56be-9821-2539b9648807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390117} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e0516-4143-5b3d-a886-9f55b545e42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e0516-4143-5b3d-a886-9f55b545e42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390118} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f89e797-de79-5d1a-816f-a2e52b045d93" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f481905-7b7b-5f76-84f8-8458f8f80c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-182669" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$6\\,∣\\,{n}^{3}+5n$ for all $n\\in N$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $6\\,∣\\,{n}^{3}+5n$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n6\\,∣\\,{n}^{3}+5n\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{1}^{3}+5(1)=6\\,\\text{and}\\,6\\,∣\\,6\n$$\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n6\\,∣\\,{k}^{3}+5k.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n6\\,∣\\,(k+1)^{3}+5(k+1).\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the expression using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is a multiple of $6.$\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${n}^{3}+5n$ is divisible by $6$ for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e1c01fd-dea0-5761-9499-d622a34fea3e" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table With Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325f06bf-f406-5fc2-86af-7118acaed061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390121} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a5e99-683b-5eb0-accd-02b9f56c0d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a5e99-683b-5eb0-accd-02b9f56c0d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390122} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5ddf07d-c622-5889-9cdd-de8fe6fc84bd" :answer-field/key "field-3" 